  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings and spatial models are based on self-reported perceptions from a survey sample in only two adjacent Midwestern cities, which may limit their direct applicability to different geographic or cultural contexts.",
      "what": "This study used focus groups, household surveys, and a participatory mapping exercise to analyze how residents in two adjacent Midwestern US cities perceive the benefits and burdens of urban birds. Using the SolVES GIS application, the researchers modeled these spatial perceptions against landscape metrics such as bird diversity, distance to water, and distance to greenspace. The resulting models mapped high- and low-priority locations for managing human-bird coexistence, contrasting valued traits like bird song and aesthetics against nuisances like waste and aggression.",
      "why": "It provides a spatially explicit method to integrate social perceptions of wildlife into urban planning, specifically focusing on racially diverse and underserved communities that are typically excluded from conservation decision-making."
    }
  },
